@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Toolchain provisioning — what is baked into the floor vs installed on demand

' purpose: which runtimes the images bake and which the seed provisions on demand,
'          and why the project's pin beats the image's copy
' covers: packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh
' covers: defs/base/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/base-node/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/base-node-lsp/Dockerfile
' How the three shared layers are assembled: _spec/defs/base-image-layers.puml.

node "proveo/base — the universal floor" as FLOOR <<host>> {
  component "git · gh · bash · jq · tmux\ndumb-init · proveo-entrypoint" as CORE <<app>>
  component "ripgrep" as RG <<app>>
  component "plantuml jar + shim\njlink JRE 72 MB · graphviz" as PUML <<app>>
  component "mise" as MISE <<app>>
}

node "proveo/base-node — the TypeScript floor" as TSFLOOR <<host>> {
  component "node 22 · pnpm · bun (+ bunx)\none of each, pinned" as TS <<app>>
}

' ── What the project PINS, the sandbox honours ───────────────────────────────
' node/pnpm come from the project, not the image. The image ships one of each; a
' repo pins its own. Measured on the pluvo monorepo: package.json pins
' "pnpm@9.15.0" and engines.pnpm "9.x" while the image carries pnpm 10.33.0 — a
' major out, enough to rewrite a lockfile. node was fine (22.23.2 satisfies
' "22.x"), so the mismatch an operator SEES as "node --version is wrong" is
' usually the package manager. corepack activates `packageManager` (it exists for
' exactly that and ships with node); mise provisions node only when the running
' one FAILS engines.node, since a range is not a pin and reinstalling a
' satisfying runtime buys nothing. Python already follows the same rule via
' _py_requested_version (.python-version, requires-python). Bun follows it too:
' the floor ships one (defs/base-node, release zip pinned by SHASUMS256 digest),
' and `packageManager: bun@x.y.z`, engines.bun or .bun-version are honoured
' through mise — corepack does not manage bun. A bun lockfile alone selects
' `bun install --frozen-lockfile`; it never triggers a reinstall.
'
' ── HOW A PIN IS COMPARED, AND WHAT COUNTS AS "SATISFIED" ───────────────────
' Knobs: PROVEO_NODE_TOOLCHAIN=off · PROVEO_NODE_TIMEOUT (default 180s, the
' corepack prepare bound) · PROVEO_AUTO_INSTALL_TOOLS=false gates the lot.
'
' _node_nearest_pkg walks UP from the scan root to the closest package.json, so a
' subdir scope still resolves the pin its monorepo declares.
'
' _node_json_field reads one field with `node -e`, never sed: parsing JSON with
' sed is how a trailing comma or a nested "engines" turns into a silently wrong
' version. A harness image WITHOUT node is the ordinary case (cecli ships python
' only), so the helper returns 0 when `command -v node` finds nothing — both
' callers assign it in a command substitution, and a 127 there aborted the whole
' seed under `set -euo pipefail`
' (_spec/internal/sbx/seed-node-version-abort.puml).
'
' _node_satisfies handles the range forms a package.json actually uses — "22.x",
' ">=22", "^22.1.0", "22" — by comparing MAJORS only. Anything it cannot parse is
' treated as SATISFIED, so an exotic range never triggers a pointless download.
' engines.node is acted on only when the running node FAILS it; a satisfying
' runtime is never reinstalled, because a range is not a pin.
'
' _bun_satisfies splits the two meanings apart: an exact X.Y.Z is matched
' EXACTLY, because that is what a pin means, and anything else is a range and
' follows _node_satisfies. _bun_mise_spec then turns what the project wrote into
' what mise installs — an exact pin verbatim, a range down to its leading version
' prefix ("^1.4" → 1.4), falling back to `latest`.
'
' corepack knows npm, pnpm and yarn ONLY. A `bun@…` pin handed to it buys nothing
' but a "could not activate" warning, so ensure_bun_pin takes that row through
' mise instead. packageManager is the exact, checksummed pin and corepack
' activates it globally for the container; without that the standalone pnpm the
' image ships wins on PATH and quietly uses a different major.
'
' Neither mechanism writes to the workspace: corepack caches under the agent's
' home, mise under its own share dir.
'
' ── Installing dependencies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
' A FOREIGN dependency tree is already unusable in the sandbox, so reinstalling
' costs it nothing and is the only way the agent can run anything. It DOES
' rewrite the mounted tree, which is the operator's checkout — so it is confined
' to exactly that case plus an absent tree. A tree that is already native is
' never silently replaced. PROVEO_DEPS=off skips it.
'
' Provisioning is invoked from proveo_seed (proveo_provision_toolchain), which
' BOTH backends reach: docker from the entrypoint, sbx also from setup.startup.
' A setup command's exports die with it, so the resolved PATH/VIRTUAL_ENV are
' written to the agent home's .bashrc — the agent is not a shell, but every
' command it runs is. That block is delimited by markers
' (PROVEO_RC_START/PROVEO_RC_END) and REPLACED rather than appended, through the
' same _proveo_write_block used for house rules: these files may already hold
' hand-written content, so appending would grow one without bound and
' overwriting would destroy work that is not ours.
'
' NOT A SUBSHELL, deliberately. ensure_project_tools reads the CWD (nx.json,
' go.mod, mise.toml), so proveo_provision_toolchain cd-s into the scan root and
' back — but it does not wrap the call, because the function EXPORTS GOPATH and
' PATH and on the docker backend this runs in the very process that goes on to
' exec the agent. A subshell would drop exactly what the agent needs to inherit.
' (GOROOT was in that list until `g` was retired; mise resolves it from the
' toolchain's own location now, and an explicit GOROOT would override that.)
'
' The GitHub token passed to installers is resolved GITHUB_TOKEN → GH_TOKEN →
' `gh auth token`, bounded at 5s. The last leg ends in `|| return 0` because
' `pipefail` makes an unauthenticated `gh auth token` fail the whole pipeline
' while the caller assigns it under `set -e` — and no token is a normal state.
'
' npm fetches here are bounded the same way as everything else on this path:
' --fetch-timeout=60000 --fetch-retries=1.
node "Container startup — proveo_provision_toolchain" as BOOT <<host>> {
  component "1. nx — nx.json" as T1 <<app>>
  component "2. turbo — turbo.json" as T2 <<app>>
  component "3. mise — mise.toml · .tool-versions" as T3 <<app>>
  component "4. go — go.mod · go.work · *.go" as T4 <<app>>
  component "gopls" as GOPLS <<app>>
}

cloud "npm · mise.run" as NPM <<cloud>>
cloud "go.dev/dl — reached by mise" as GODL <<cloud>>
component "Blocked egress\nstartup must not hang" as BLOCK <<error>>
component "Unreachable apt archive\nat BUILD time" as APT <<error>>

CORE ARROW_MAIN BOOT : every harness inherits the floor
FLOOR ARROW_MAIN TSFLOOR : FROM
TS ARROW_MAIN BOOT : ""ensure_node_toolchain"" — corepack for pnpm/yarn,\nmise for ""bun@"" pins and engines.node misses
TS ARROW_OPTIONAL MISE : ""mise use -g bun@<pin>"" only when\nthe shipped bun fails the project's pin
T1 ARROW_CLOUD NPM
T2 ARROW_CLOUD NPM
T3 ARROW_CLOUD NPM
T4 ARROW_MAIN T3 : ""mise use -g go@<version>"" — the only Go path
T3 ARROW_CLOUD GODL
T4 ARROW_MAIN GOPLS : only once a toolchain exists
BOOT ARROW_ERROR BLOCK : bounded timeouts · ""PROVEO_AUTO_INSTALL_TOOLS=false""
CORE ARROW_ERROR APT : ""APT::Update::Error-Mode=any"" — fail at the fetch, not 15 layers later

note right of PUML
  **Why plantuml is on the floor.** This fleet's specs //are// ""\.puml""
  (see ""_spec/""), so an agent that cannot run ""plantuml -checkonly""
  cannot verify the artifact it just edited. Shipped as the upstream
  jar behind a shell shim — the Debian package pulls a much larger
  dependency set for the same jar. A Java runtime and graphviz are its
  prerequisites; graphviz renders every non-sequence diagram.

  The runtime is no longer a distro package. ""default-jre-headless""
  was 194 MB (dpkg Installed-Size 198,806 kB) on all 14 images for one
  jar; a ""jlink"" runtime built in a discarded stage is **72 MB** and
  renders ""-tpng"" and ""-tsvg"" identically. Its module set is
  deliberately larger than plantuml needs, because jdtls has to be
  hostable on it — see ""_spec/defs/base-image-layers\.puml"".
end note

note bottom of TSFLOOR
  **Bun rides the TypeScript floor, not a harness.** The seed
  already chose ""bun install --frozen-lockfile"" for a
  ""bun.lock"" tree and then reported "bun is not on PATH" on every
  Bun project. One pinned binary in ""base-node"" reaches opencode,
  claudecode and every ""-browser"" variant; cursor and cecli stay
  runtime-free by design.
end note

note right of MISE
  **Why mise is on the floor.** It is the toolchain manager this repo
  already uses on the host — now for Go too, which makes it the ONLY
  one. With it present an agent can provision what a task needs
  (""mise use go@1.26"") instead of every image pre-baking every runtime.

  **mise is also the task runner, and it picks the interpreter from a
  shebang.** No shebang means ""sh"". A bash task without one parses
  under dash until the first bashism, so it fails PART WAY THROUGH:
  ""test-images"" staged eight cross-compiled binaries and then died on
  ""tests=("" with ""sh: 4: Syntax error"", which reads as a broken test
  script rather than a wrong interpreter. Guarded by
  ""internal/contract"" ""TestMiseTasksUsingBashSyntaxDeclareBash"".
end note

note bottom of T4
  **Presence is not correctness.** The tool home is durable and GLOBAL, so the Go
  installed for one project is still on PATH in the next one. Checking only
  ""command -v go"" let a repo pinning ""toolchain go1.26.0"" build silently on
  whatever version an earlier workspace happened to install. The resolved
  version (""go env GOVERSION"") is compared against the pin, and only an explicit
  pin triggers a reinstall — with no pin any working toolchain is the right one,
  and chasing "latest" every boot would re-download for nothing.
end note

note bottom of T3
  **Installers are serialized across runs.** The tool home is one directory shared
  by every run on the machine — since the relocation, by runs on BOTH backends —
  so two harnesses starting together race on the same ""npm -g --prefix"" tree and
  mise dir, which corrupts rather than duplicates. An ""flock"" guards both installer sections; failing to lock is not
  failing to boot (no flock → no locking, timeout → skip installs, since the run
  holding the lock is installing the same things).
end note

note bottom of T4
  **Go is detected, never baked.** A toolchain is ~250MB that most
  workspaces never touch, so it follows the same
  detect-then-provision pattern as nx/turbo/mise rather than becoming
  a Docker layer.

  **One manager, not two.** ""g"" (github.com/stefanmaric/g) held this
  slot and is retired. Not for failing to install — measured inside an
  sbx sandbox it fetches and unpacks go1.26.5 fine — but because
  installing is ALL it does. It writes a ""GOROOT"" and stops, leaving
  every consumer to find that directory through a shell rc.

  A sandbox has no persistent rc to write one into: no
  ""/etc/sandbox-persistent\.sh"", no ""CLAUDE_ENV_FILE"". So a correct
  toolchain sat on disk while ""mise run <task>"" answered
  ""go: command not found"". mise's shims are on PATH from
  ""_proveo_tool_path"" on every backend, which is the half ""g"" never had.

  Two smaller edges the swap closed. ""g"" refuses outright unless
  ""$GOPATH/bin"" is ALREADY on PATH ("...and it is required"), and the
  call site discarded that message — the operator read only
  ""WARN: g could not install Go"", naming neither cause nor fix. And
  ""GOROOT"" is no longer exported by proveo at all: an explicit one
  overrides what the go binary infers from its own path, so the value
  ""g"" needed would now aim a mise toolchain at an empty directory.

  The repo's own pin is honoured when present (""toolchain goX.Y.Z"" in
  ""go\.mod""), so the container resolves the same toolchain as CI and
  the developer's host — and ""mise\.toml"" carries the identical pin so
  ""mise run"" on the HOST resolves it too, which is the case that had no
  answer while Go was absent from that file.
end note

note bottom of GOPLS
  The shared LSP detector already maps ""go\.mod"" and ""\.go"" to gopls,
  so installing it here is what switches Go code intelligence on for
  every LSP-capable harness.
end note

note right of BOOT
  **What this step deliberately does NOT do: install the project's own
  dependencies.** No ""pnpm install"", no ""pip install -r"". The entrypoint is
  a fail-fast gate that assumes the image already ships the runtimes and
  toolchains it promises; the agent installs what the repo needs, on demand,
  once it can see the repo. Prefetching them here would make every start
  pay for a tree the session may never touch, and would hide a missing
  runtime behind a successful-looking install.
end note


note bottom of BLOCK
  Every fetch is time-bounded so a blackholed egress cannot hang the
  container at startup, and the whole installer is opt-out for
  locked-egress deployments. Installers are downloaded before being
  run: piping a fetch straight into ""sh"" masks the failure, because an
  empty body still exits 0.
end note

note bottom of APT
  **The same masked failure, one phase earlier.** ""apt-get update"" only
  //warns// on an unreachable archive and exits 0. The build then walks on
  and dies at the next layer with ""E: Unable to locate package <x>"",
  which reads like a misspelled package rather than a network outage —
  it sent one Fedora deploy failure looking at the Dockerfile while the
  fault was DNS inside the buildkit container
  (""_spec/_devops/buildx-driver-selection\.puml"").

  ""-o APT::Update::Error-Mode=any"" turns those warnings into errors, so
  the build stops at the fetch that actually failed, naming the host it
  could not resolve. It also closes a quieter hole: without it apt
  installs from a //stale// index, silently pinning an image to whatever
  the last reachable mirror said.

  Every ""apt-get update"" under ""defs/"" carries the flag. ""base-node"" is
  the exception — NodeSource's setup script runs its own update, and
  that layer already begins with ""curl -fsSL"", which fails hard.
end note
@enduml
